            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical theology, justice is primarily understood as God's righteous character and moral order applied to relationships, encompassing both restorative action (vindicating the oppressed, protecting the vulnerable) and retributive action (punishing wrongdoing).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Justice is defined specifically as the application of righteousness to right a wronged neighbor, highlighting its restorative and relational nature.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's moral order and righteousness manifested in equitable treatment, restorative action, and judicial retribution.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Righteousness applied specifically to restore or vindicate a neighbor who has been wronged.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book narrows the definition to the restorative and relational aspect of justice (righting a wronged neighbor), omitting the broader retributive (punishing sin) and strictly legal/forensic dimensions included in the consensus.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book identifies a central, widely recognized facet of biblical justice but treats it as the exclusive or defining focus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus broadly identifies justice as encompassing both restorative action for the oppressed and retributive judgment against evil. The book limits its definition strictly to the restorative aspect of applying righteousness to help a wronged neighbor, refining the concept into a narrower subset.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The biblical concept of justice (mishpat) frequently includes punitive judgment against the wicked (e.g., Romans 2:5-8, Revelation 19:2), which goes beyond merely restoring a wronged neighbor.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
